\begin{abstract}
Modular LLM agents that route between neural reasoning and deterministic computation promise improved reliability, yet understanding \emph{what} drives the value of routing decisions remains unclear. We introduce a formal diagnostic framework---episode-level counterfactual analysis with the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF) metric---that decomposes routing signal into death-avoidance and non-death components by running complete episodes under pure strategies and comparing final outcomes. The framework is environment-agnostic, requiring only paired episode outcomes under two modalities. We demonstrate this in environments with catastrophic failure modes. Applied to ScienceWorld (11 of 30 task types, 245 episodes, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), \textbf{78.9\% of non-tie routing labels} (75 of 95, penalty-independent) are driven by death asymmetry; under ScienceWorld's actual penalty ($P{=}100$), this corresponds to DAF $= 90.4\%$ (95\% CI: $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$, score-level, $P$-dependent). Per-task death rate correlates with routing signal ($r{=}0.97$), though this is largely a mathematical consequence of death dominance (partial $r{=}0.02$, $p{=}0.91$ after controlling for $\delta \cdot P$; \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}). An exploratory router yields an informative negative: episode-level balanced accuracy remains at or below chance at practical scales (0.6B--1.7B) and reaches only $59.0\%$ at 4B ($p < 0.005$), a disproportionate cost for $+9$\,pp above random---validating the analytical framework over brute-force learned routing. Cross-environment analysis on MATH ($N{=}300$) shows the death-driven routing signal is not statistically detectable without a death penalty ($+4.3$~pp, $p = 0.079$, not reaching $\alpha{=}0.05$). A Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct replication (7 task types, 70 episodes) confirms direction-consistent death patterns for all task types ($+34.3\%$ vs.\ $+26.6\%$ death-rate differential). These findings indicate that in environments with catastrophic failure modes, routing value is dominated by safety---the routing problem reduces to safety-first modality selection rather than efficiency optimization.
\end{abstract}

