\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task scatter: death rate differential (internal $-$ deterministic) vs.\ performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). $r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$, $n = 11$ task types.}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

Across 245 episodes, $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$; excluding death episodes, $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$, yielding DAF $= 90.4\%$ (95\% CI: $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$; at default $P{=}100$). The penalty-independent death-asymmetry label fraction is 78.9\% (75 of 95 non-tie labels). The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes versus 16.3\% for deterministic (2.6$\times$). Per-task, death rate differential correlates with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ ($r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$; Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}). However, when $P$ is constant across tasks, $\text{Corr}(P \cdot \delta + \Delta_{\text{nd}},\; \delta) \rightarrow 1$ as DAF $\rightarrow 1$; controlling for $\delta \cdot P$, the residual is $r = 0.02$ ($p = 0.91$, $df = 27$). We retain $r = 0.97$ as an internal consistency check, not as independent evidence for death avoidance. The correlation drops below significance at $P \leq 25$ (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Of 245 episodes, 86 are \texttt{det\_preferred}, 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}.

\paragraph{Signal concentration.}
The SCI (\S\ref{sec:sci}) over per-task routing signal contributions is $0.2147$ (low concentration): the top-3 task types account for only $39.8\%$ of total death-rate differential. One outlier, \texttt{find-non-living-thing}, has zero deaths (DAF $= 0\%$, $\Delta = +9.58$ from non-death scores); all 10 remaining types exhibit DAF $= 100\%$. Death avoidance is systematic, not driven by a few outlier tasks.

\paragraph{Bootstrap confidence intervals.}
Episode-level bootstrap resampling ($B = 10{,}000$) yields a 95\% CI of $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$ for the aggregate DAF, excluding both 50\% and 100\%. This CI quantifies within-sample uncertainty; generalization to unseen task types requires held-out evaluation. Per-task CIs confirm $\Delta > 0$ for 10 of 11 types; \texttt{chemistry-mix} ($N{=}20$, CI $[-5.0, 48.6]$) is the sole exception, attributable to small sample size.

\subsection{Multi-Model Validation}
\label{sec:cross-model}

A Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct replication on 7 task types ($N{=}70$) shows the death-avoidance pattern reproduces and intensifies: internal death rate $34.3\%$ vs.\ deterministic $0\%$ (cf.\ Qwen: $42.9\%$ vs.\ $16.3\%$), with zero internal-preferred episodes (24 det-preferred, 46 no-preference). With $N{=}70$ and zero internal-preferred labels, this demonstrates directional consistency of the death mechanism across model families, not full framework replication---which would require a larger sample and independent environment (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}).

\subsection{Cross-Domain Negative Contrast: MATH}
\label{sec:math-contrast}

On MATH Level~4--5 (300 problems, code interpreter as deterministic modality), the routing signal inverts: internal reasoning achieves $81.0\%$ vs.\ $76.7\%$ ($+4.3$~pp, McNemar $p = 0.079$, not reaching $\alpha{=}0.05$). The label distribution likewise inverts: 30 \texttt{int\_preferred} vs.\ 17 \texttt{det\_preferred} (cf.\ 9:86 in ScienceWorld), with 84.3\% \texttt{no\_preference}. Without a death penalty, death-avoidance signal is absent as our framework predicts. A post-hoc power analysis indicates this evaluation was underpowered (power $= 0.42$; 80\% power requires $N{\approx}770$~\citep{lakens-2017}); we interpret the $+4.3$~pp trend as directional evidence, not a definitive finding.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router$^\dagger$ & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 tasks, 245 episodes). $\dagger$\,Single seed; see \S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points. The oracle improves only to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of decisions matching always-deterministic---\emph{oracle degeneracy} indicating the optimal routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation.

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic Probe}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic of label informativeness. An implicit router (Qwen3-1.7B fine-tuned; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) yields $54.1 \pm 5.4\%$ step-level balanced accuracy but $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$ on held-out episodes (8 seeds)---below always-deterministic (50\%). Capacity scaling is non-monotonic (0.6B: $50.7\%$, 1.7B: $47.1\%$, 4B: $59.0\%$; configuration differences---8 vs.\ 4 seeds---may contribute); only 4B exceeds the baseline ($58.96 \pm 1.94\%$, $p < 0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}). Augmentation-based data scaling ($25\%$--$800\%$) provides no improvement (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}), but paraphrase augmentation inflates the majority class (89.6\%$\rightarrow$97.7\%) without generating novel routing patterns; real-data scaling---requiring ${\sim}1{,}350$ episode pairs for 50 minority-class examples---remains an open question. Notably, capacity scaling improves episode-level routing ($+9$\,pp at 4B) without improving step-level accuracy (flat ${\sim}53\%$), confirming that the extractable signal is task-type-level structure. The cost of this extraction (4B for $+9$\,pp) validates our analytical methodology over brute-force learned routing. Deployed end-to-end (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}), the router ($-10.70$) does not outperform always-deterministic ($-9.47$; overlapping CIs). Label quality is verified in Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}.

Failure mode and ablation details in Appendices~\ref{app:failure-modes}--\ref{app:ablations}.

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tool Use and External Computation in LLMs.}
A growing body of work augments language models with external tools \citep{tool-learning-survey-2024}.
Toolformer \cite{toolformer-2023} teaches LLMs to insert API calls via self-supervised loss filtering; ToolkenGPT \cite{toolkengpt-2023} represents tools as special tokens enabling frozen models to invoke massive tool libraries; ART \cite{art-2023} decomposes tasks into tool-augmented programs; Chameleon \cite{chameleon-2023} composes heterogeneous modules per subtask; ReTool \cite{retool-2025} applies RL to discover when code execution is preferable to chain-of-thought.
These focus on \emph{learning to use tools}; we ask a prior question: \emph{what signal drives the routing decision?}
Recent concurrent work sharpens this question: \citet{tool-overuse-illusion-2026} identify a cognitive-illusion effect in LLM tool use, \citet{when2tool-2026} show tool-call decisions are encoded in hidden states before reasoning, \citet{tool-use-tax-2026} quantify a ``tool-use tax'' where tools degrade performance, and \citet{to-call-or-not-2026} propose a necessity--utility--affordability framework.
Our counterfactual decomposition provides environment-grounded evidence: in ScienceWorld, $90.4\%$ of routing signal at $P{=}100$ traces to catastrophic-failure prevention.

\paragraph{Adaptive Computation and Routing.}
CogRouter \cite{cogrouter-2026} adapts reasoning depth per step, ARES \cite{ares-2026} selects among reasoning effort levels, Mixture-of-Recursions \cite{mixture-of-recursions-2025} learns token-level recursion depths, and MTRouter \cite{mtrouter-2026} addresses multi-turn routing across heterogeneous models.
RouteLLM \citep{routellm-2024} learns routers from preference data to select between strong and weak models.
These optimize \emph{how} to route; we reveal \emph{why} routing helps---in interactive environments, the dominant signal is catastrophic-failure avoidance.

\paragraph{Counterfactual Evaluation in RL.}
Prior work generates counterfactual explanations for RL agents by perturbing individual actions within a fixed policy \citep{olson2021counterfactual, gajcin2023counterfactual, amitai2024explaining}.
Our approach differs in both purpose and granularity: we execute two complete episodes under different routing strategies, producing prospective paired comparisons that isolate routing signal \emph{before} any router is trained, sidestepping compounding perturbation artifacts.

\paragraph{Safe RL and Catastrophic Failure.}
\citet{garcia2015comprehensive} survey safe RL methods that constrain policies to avoid catastrophic outcomes; \citet{crpo-2021} develop principled constrained optimization with formal convergence guarantees.
Our counterfactual decomposition reveals that death avoidance is the dominant component of routing signal, suggesting that modality routing functions primarily as a safety mechanism rather than a generic capability-matching problem.

\paragraph{LLM Agents in Interactive Environments.}
ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022} serves as our primary testbed; ALFWorld \cite{alfworld-2021} provides a complementary household environment; Voyager \cite{voyager-2023} demonstrates open-ended exploration in Minecraft using LLM-driven curriculum.
ReAct \cite{react-2023} interleaves reasoning with actions and serves as our internal baseline; Reflexion \cite{reflexion-2023} extends this with verbal self-reflection.
Recent agent architectures augment planning with world models \citep{itp-2026, dynathink-2025}, which inherit hallucination risks.
Our deterministic modality provides exact state transitions, and our analysis shows this exactness matters almost exclusively for death avoidance.

